---
import Button from"../../components/ui/Button.astro";

import MedicalNote from"../../components/ui/MedicalNote.astro";
import ContentLayout from"../../layouts/ContentLayout.astro";
import type { FaqItem } from"../../lib/seo";
import ArticleImage from"../../components/ui/ArticleImage.astro";

const faqs: FaqItem[] = [
  { q:"What is a calorie surplus?", a:"A calorie surplus means eating more calories than your body burns. Combined with resistance training, the extra energy supports muscle growth and weight gain." },
  { q:"How big should a calorie surplus be?", a:"A surplus of 250–500 calories per day supports lean muscle gain. Larger surpluses add more fat without building muscle any faster." },
];
---
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8" />
  <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0" />
  <title>Calorie Surplus: The Complete Guide to Gaining Muscle Mass</title>
  <meta name="description" content="Discover what a calorie surplus is, how big it should be, and how to use it strategically to build muscle mass without accumulating excess fat. All backed by science." />
  <link rel="canonical" href="https://kirocalorie.com/guides/calorie-surplus-guide.html" />
  <link rel="alternate" hreflang="en" href="https://kirocalorie.com/guides/calorie-surplus-guide.html" />
  <script src="https://cdn.tailwindcss.com"></script>
</head>
<body class="bg-[#f8fafc] text-ink font-sans antialiased selection:bg-blue-200 selection:text-ink">
  <main class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 py-16 md:py-24">
    
    <!-- Hero Header -->
    <header class="mb-16 text-center">
      <div class="inline-block bg-brand-soft text-ink font-bold px-5 py-2 rounded-full text-sm tracking-wider mb-8 uppercase shadow-sm">
        Sports Nutrition & Fitness
      </div>
      <h1 class="text-4xl md:text-6xl font-extrabold tracking-tight text-ink mb-8 bg-clip-text text-transparent bg-gradient-to-r from-blue-700 to-indigo-600 leading-tight">
        Calorie Surplus: The Ultimate Guide
      </h1>
      <p class="text-xl md:text-2xl text-body font-light max-w-3xl mx-auto leading-relaxed">
        To build real muscle you need extra fuel. Discover how to calculate and apply a structured calorie surplus (Bulking) that adds mostly lean muscle mass and minimizes body fat accumulation.
      </p>
    </header>

    <div class="mb-12">
      <MedicalNote />
    </div>
    
    <div class="my-12 rounded-[2rem] overflow-hidden shadow-2xl border border-hairline bg-surface p-4">
      <div class="rounded-[1.5rem] overflow-hidden">
        <ArticleImage src="/assets/images/articles/muscle-gain.svg" alt="Illustration of muscle gain and calorie surplus" />
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Main Content -->
    <article class="space-y-16 text-lg md:text-xl text-body leading-relaxed font-light">
      
      <section>
        <p class="mb-6 first-letter:text-7xl first-letter:font-black first-letter:text-ink first-letter:mr-3 first-letter:float-left first-letter:leading-none">
          While the vast majority of the fitness industry, fad diets, and the media are almost obsessively focused on weight loss and leaning out, building real, significant muscle mass requires the exact opposite approach: entering a <strong class="font-bold text-ink">calorie surplus</strong>.
        </p>
        <p class="mb-6">
          Also popularly known in gym culture as a"bulking phase," a calorie surplus occurs when you intentionally and strategically consume more energy (calories) from food and drinks than your body burns to maintain its vital functions and daily activity levels.
        </p>
        <p class="mb-6">
          Think of your body as a contractor building a house. Your biological system is extremely efficient and conservative. Building and maintaining additional muscle tissue is metabolically very expensive (it requires burning a lot of energy constantly). If your body does not have an abundance of building materials (proteins and amino acids) and spare energy (calories in the form of carbohydrates and fats), it will always prioritize basic survival. Without that excess energy, the body will refuse to approve the"construction" of new biceps or quads, no matter how hard you train. Systematically providing this energy excess is the non-negotiable foundation of lean mass gain.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="bg-surface p-8 md:p-14 rounded-[2.5rem] shadow-sm border border-hairline">
        <h2 class="text-3xl md:text-5xl font-extrabold text-ink mb-8 tracking-tight">The Biology of Muscle Hypertrophy</h2>
        <p class="mb-6">
          There is a very true adage in the training world:"Muscle doesn't grow in the gym, it gets torn in the gym; it grows while you sleep and eat." When you lift heavy weights or subject your muscles to high-intensity resistance training close to failure, you cause mechanical stress, tension, and metabolic stress in the muscle fibers. This creates what is microscopically known as <em>micro-tears</em>.
        </p>
        <p class="mb-6">
          The actual muscle growth process is called <strong>Muscle Protein Synthesis (MPS)</strong>. After an intense workout, your body seeks to repair these micro-tears to make the fibers thicker and stronger, anticipating that you will subject them to that stress again in the future. But for the end result to be a net increase in muscle, the rate of Protein Synthesis must be greater than the rate of Muscle Protein Breakdown (MPB). For this balance to be positive, biology dictates that you need:
        </p>
        <ul class="list-none space-y-6 mb-8">
          <li class="flex items-start">
            <span class="flex-shrink-0 w-8 h-8 rounded-full bg-brand-soft text-brand flex items-center justify-center font-bold mr-4 mt-1">✓</span>
            <div>
              <strong class="block text-ink text-xl mb-1">A Positive Nitrogen Balance</strong>
              <p class="text-body text-base">Provided through an adequate and sustained intake of high-quality dietary proteins, which provide all essential amino acids (especially Leucine), the true building blocks of muscle.</p>
            </div>
          </li>
          <li class="flex items-start">
            <span class="flex-shrink-0 w-8 h-8 rounded-full bg-brand-soft text-brand flex items-center justify-center font-bold mr-4 mt-1">✓</span>
            <div>
              <strong class="block text-ink text-xl mb-1">Energy Abundance (ATP)</strong>
              <p class="text-body text-base">Protein synthesis is a biological process that consumes massive amounts of energy. The body needs extra calories not only to sustain your hard workouts, but to"pay the metabolic cost" of fusing amino acids into new contractile protein chains.</p>
            </div>
          </li>
        </ul>
        <div class="bg-brand-soft/50 p-6 rounded-2xl border border-hairline/50 text-base">
          <strong class="text-ink">Scientific Note:</strong> A calorie surplus doesn't just provide passive energy. An excess of calories stimulates a highly anabolic hormonal environment, elevating levels of Insulin (the body's most anabolic hormone, pushing nutrients into cells) and Insulin-like Growth Factor 1 (IGF-1), critical signals the body needs to know it's in a time of"abundance" and that it's safe and favorable to build muscle tissue.
        </div>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2 class="text-3xl md:text-5xl font-extrabold text-ink mb-10 tracking-tight">Different Approaches: Lean Bulk vs. Dirty Bulk</h2>
        <p class="mb-8">
          If having more energy is good for muscle growth, you might ask yourself: <em>"Wouldn't it be better to eat the maximum amount of food possible to grow as fast as possible?"</em> The resounding answer from modern physiology is: <strong>No.</strong>
        </p>
        <p class="mb-8">
          The primary goal of a smart bulking phase (or structured surplus) is to maximize the rate of hypertrophy while minimizing, as much as possible, the accumulation of adipose tissue (body fat). The human body has a biological and genetic rate limit at which it can synthesize new muscle tissue. Depending on your experience level (beginner, intermediate, advanced) and your genetics, you can gain between 0.5 and 2 pounds of pure muscle a month. Eating excessive amounts of pizzas, ice cream, and burgers (generating a massive surplus) won't speed up muscle creation beyond that limit. Any excess the body can't use to repair muscle will inevitably be stored as fat.
        </p>

        <div class="overflow-x-auto rounded-[2rem] shadow-xl border border-hairline mb-10 bg-surface">
          <table class="w-full text-left border-collapse min-w-[700px]">
            <thead>
              <tr class="bg-surface-elevated text-white">
                <th class="p-6 text-lg font-semibold border-b border-hairline">Bulking Type</th>
                <th class="p-6 text-lg font-semibold border-b border-hairline">Daily Surplus</th>
                <th class="p-6 text-lg font-semibold border-b border-hairline">Average Monthly Gain</th>
                <th class="p-6 text-lg font-semibold border-b border-hairline">Results and Effects</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody class="text-base text-body divide-y divide-slate-100">
              <tr class="hover:bg-canvas-soft transition-colors">
                <td class="p-6 font-bold text-brand text-lg">Lean Bulk</td>
                <td class="p-6 font-medium">+ 200 to 300 kcal</td>
                <td class="p-6">1.5 - 2.5 lbs (0.7 - 1.2 kg)</td>
                <td class="p-6 leading-relaxed">Optimal muscle growth. Fat accumulation is minimal. Highly recommended because you can maintain this phase for many months without needing to cut, maximizing anabolic time.</td>
              </tr>
              <tr class="hover:bg-canvas-soft transition-colors">
                <td class="p-6 font-bold text-ink text-lg">Standard Bulk</td>
                <td class="p-6 font-medium">+ 500 kcal</td>
                <td class="p-6">3.0 - 4.0 lbs (1.4 - 1.8 kg)</td>
                <td class="p-6 leading-relaxed">Good muscle growth, but moderate to significant fat gain. It's the"classic" bodybuilding route that forces a strict cutting phase shortly after to lose the extra baggage.</td>
              </tr>
              <tr class="hover:bg-canvas-soft transition-colors">
                <td class="p-6 font-bold text-red-600 text-lg">Dirty Bulk</td>
                <td class="p-6 font-medium">+ 1,000+ kcal</td>
                <td class="p-6">Over 6.0 lbs (2.7+ kg)</td>
                <td class="p-6 leading-relaxed">Dangerous in the medium term. Generates the maximum possible muscle gain (with no real difference from standard), but carries massive amounts of fat. Frequently produces insulin sensitivity deterioration (which depresses anabolism) and constant lethargy.</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="relative bg-gradient-to-br from-blue-900 to-indigo-900 text-white p-8 md:p-14 rounded-[3rem] shadow-2xl overflow-hidden">
        <div class="absolute inset-0 opacity-10 bg-[url('data:image/svg+xml;base64,PHN2ZyB3aWR0aD0iNDAiIGhlaWdodD0iNDAiIHhtbG5zPSJodHRwOi8vd3d3LnczLm9yZy8yMDAwL3N2ZyI+PGNpcmNsZSBjeD0iMjAiIGN5PSIyMCIgcj0iMiIgZmlsbD0iI2ZmZmZmZiIvPjwvc3ZnPg==')]"></div>
        
        <div class="relative z-10">
          <h2 class="text-3xl md:text-5xl font-extrabold mb-8 tracking-tight">Step by Step: How to Calculate Your Perfect Surplus</h2>
          <p class="mb-10 text-blue-100 text-lg md:text-xl font-light max-w-3xl">The number one mistake gym-goers make is guessing how much they eat. If you guess, failure is almost guaranteed. The calculation requires precise math based on your physical characteristics and lifestyle.</p>
          
          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-surface/10 backdrop-blur-lg border border-white/20 p-6 md:p-8 rounded-2xl flex flex-col md:flex-row gap-6 items-center md:items-start">
              <div class="w-16 h-16 rounded-full bg-brand text-white font-black text-2xl flex items-center justify-center flex-shrink-0 shadow-lg">1</div>
              <div>
                <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold mb-2">Calculate Your Basal Metabolic Rate (BMR) and TDEE</h3>
                <p class="text-blue-100 leading-relaxed font-light">Before adding calories, you must know your maintenance"baseline." Your TDEE (Total Daily Energy Expenditure) is the sum of the calories you burn just existing (BMR), plus the thermic effect of food (TEF), plus your non-exercise activity thermogenesis (NEAT), plus the exercise you do. Use an advanced online calculator to get this number accurately.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
            
            <div class="bg-surface/10 backdrop-blur-lg border border-white/20 p-6 md:p-8 rounded-2xl flex flex-col md:flex-row gap-6 items-center md:items-start">
              <div class="w-16 h-16 rounded-full bg-brand text-white font-black text-2xl flex items-center justify-center flex-shrink-0 shadow-lg">2</div>
              <div>
                <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold mb-2">Add Your Surplus Margin (+250 kcal)</h3>
                <p class="text-blue-100 leading-relaxed font-light">Take your maintenance figure (TDEE) obtained in step one and add exactly between 200 and 300 calories. For example, if you burn 2600 calories daily, your lean bulk calorie target will be around 2850 calories. It's that simple.</p>
              </div>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-surface/10 backdrop-blur-lg border border-white/20 p-6 md:p-8 rounded-2xl flex flex-col md:flex-row gap-6 items-center md:items-start">
              <div class="w-16 h-16 rounded-full bg-brand text-white font-black text-2xl flex items-center justify-center flex-shrink-0 shadow-lg">3</div>
              <div>
                <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold mb-2">Macronutrient Distribution (Macros)</h3>
                <p class="text-blue-100 leading-relaxed font-light mb-4">Not all calories are equal in a bodybuilding context. Once you have your total calories, divide them as follows, based on current sports physiology:</p>
                <ul class="list-disc pl-5 text-blue-50 space-y-2 font-light">
                  <li><strong>Proteins:</strong> Set a fixed target of 0.8 to 1.0 grams of protein per pound of body weight (1.8 to 2.2 grams per kg). This provides the structure.</li>
                  <li><strong>Fats:</strong> Adjust fats around 0.35 to 0.45 grams per pound (0.8 to 1.0 grams per kg). Essential for keeping your testosterone production high.</li>
                  <li><strong>Carbohydrates:</strong> Assign absolutely all remaining calories to carbohydrates. They are your explosive energy source for training, they refill glycogen, and they generate the insulin spike necessary for anabolism.</li>
                </ul>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2 class="text-3xl md:text-5xl font-extrabold text-ink mb-8 tracking-tight">The"Hardgainer" Phenomenon: Strategies for Ectomorphs</h2>
        <p class="mb-6">
          For certain people with hyper-fast adaptive metabolisms, high spontaneous activity (NEAT), and a natural ectomorph somatotype, eating in a constant surplus is a physical and mental challenge. If you are a"Hardgainer" (someone who struggles tremendously to gain weight), you know that eating 3500 calories of chicken breast, broccoli, and rice is unsustainable and will cause massive gastric distress.
        </p>
        <p class="mb-10">Nutrition science recommends applying calorie density strategies to trick your stomach's satiety signals and easily reach your calories:</p>
        
        <div class="grid md:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-3 gap-8">
          <div class="bg-surface p-8 rounded-[2rem] border border-hairline shadow-lg hover:shadow-xl transition-shadow duration-300">
            <div class="text-5xl mb-6">🥤</div>
            <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-ink mb-3">Drink Your Calories</h3>
            <p class="text-base text-body leading-relaxed">The gastric emptying of liquids is extremely fast. Blended foods bypass the brain's hormonal satiety signals. Prepare a daily shake: 300ml whole milk, 1 scoop whey protein, 1 banana, 50g oats, and 2 tablespoons peanut butter. That's nearly 800 nutritious calories you'll drink in minutes without feeling heavy.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-surface p-8 rounded-[2rem] border border-hairline shadow-lg hover:shadow-xl transition-shadow duration-300">
            <div class="text-5xl mb-6">🥑</div>
            <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-ink mb-3">Maximize Energy Density</h3>
            <p class="text-base text-body leading-relaxed">100 grams of broccoli has 34 calories. 100 grams of walnuts has 600 calories, and they take up the same space in your stomach. Generously use extra virgin olive oil on your meals, eat avocados, nuts, dark chocolate, and swap lean cuts for higher-calorie meat like chicken thighs, salmon, or beef.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-surface p-8 rounded-[2rem] border border-hairline shadow-lg hover:shadow-xl transition-shadow duration-300">
            <div class="text-5xl mb-6">⏱️</div>
            <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-ink mb-3">Increase Frequency</h3>
            <p class="text-base text-body leading-relaxed">Don't try to eat gargantuan volumes in your three classic meals. It's preferable to have 4 or 5 moderate meals separated by 3 hours. You'll maintain constant protein synthesis in the blood (Aminoacidemia) and avoid the lethargy caused by brutal digestion.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="bg-surface p-8 md:p-14 rounded-[2.5rem] shadow-sm border border-hairline">
        <h2 class="text-3xl md:text-5xl font-extrabold text-ink mb-8 tracking-tight">Metrics & Tracking: How to Know if Your Bulk is Working</h2>
        <p class="mb-6">
          A rookie mistake is to simply"eat a lot" and assume all the weight gained is lean mass. Scientific fitness requires meticulous tracking. If your goal is to minimize extra fat accumulation, you need to measure your progress using multiple vectors, since the scale alone lies regularly due to glycogen and water retention.
        </p>
        <ul class="space-y-8 mt-10">
          <li class="relative pl-10">
            <span class="absolute left-0 top-1 text-brand font-bold text-2xl">•</span>
            <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-ink mb-2">1. Weekly Moving Average on the Scale</h3>
            <p class="text-body font-light leading-relaxed">
              Don't look at your weight on a random day. Weigh yourself under identical conditions: 3 or 4 mornings a week, immediately upon waking up, after using the bathroom, and before ingesting liquids. Add the values and divide by the number of days. Your goal is for that weekly average to go up by about 0.2 to 0.5 lbs (100 to 250 grams). If the average doesn't go up for two consecutive weeks, your body has metabolically adapted (adaptive thermogenesis) and your old surplus has become your new maintenance level. You must add 150 extra calories to your daily macros.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li class="relative pl-10">
            <span class="absolute left-0 top-1 text-brand font-bold text-2xl">•</span>
            <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-ink mb-2">2. Strength Progression (Progressive Overload)</h3>
            <p class="text-body font-light leading-relaxed">
              The calorie surplus is just the bricks and mortar. If you don't tell the body where to build via force, nothing will happen. You should feel like you have colossal energy levels, and your performance on compound lifts (Squat, Deadlift, Bench Press) should skyrocket. If your body weight increases but your gym numbers are stalled, it's highly likely you're gaining almost exclusively fat. Muscle moves weight; fat is inert tissue.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li class="relative pl-10">
            <span class="absolute left-0 top-1 text-brand font-bold text-2xl">•</span>
            <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-ink mb-2">3. Anthropometric Measurements and the Mirror</h3>
            <p class="text-body font-light leading-relaxed">
              Take photos of your physique once every two weeks in the same lighting. Use a flexible body tape measure. The goal is for the measurement of your arms, chest, back, and legs to grow faster than the circumference of your waist and abdomen. A slight waist increase is normal (your digestive organs are processing a larger volume of food), but a drastic increase in the waist indicates a brutal excess of fat, and is a sign to immediately drop 200 calories.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2 class="text-3xl md:text-5xl font-extrabold text-ink mb-10 tracking-tight">Absurd (But Dangerous) Myths Debunked by Science</h2>
        <div class="grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-8">
          <div class="bg-surface p-8 rounded-[2rem] shadow-md border-t-4 border-red-500">
            <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-ink mb-4">Myth:"Fat turns into muscle"</h3>
            <p class="text-body font-light leading-relaxed">
              <strong>False.</strong> Physiology doesn't work like alchemy. Fat (adipose tissue) and muscle (protein and contractile tissue) are two completely separate and independent biological entities. It would be like trying to turn wood into steel. You must lose the fat using a calorie deficit and build the muscle with a surplus.
            </p>
          </div>
          
          <div class="bg-surface p-8 rounded-[2rem] shadow-md border-t-4 border-red-500">
            <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-ink mb-4">Myth:"Eating infinite protein will make me huge"</h3>
            <p class="text-body font-light leading-relaxed">
              <strong>False.</strong> Modern science (such as studies by Morton et al., 2018) overwhelmingly shows that protein synthesis hits a"ceiling" between 1.6g and 2.2g per kilo of body weight (0.7-1g per lb). Beyond that, excess protein is simply converted into energy via gluconeogenesis or excreted in the urine. It will not build an extra gram of muscle.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

    </article>

    <!-- FAQ Section Integrated -->
    <section class="mt-24 bg-surface-elevated text-white p-8 md:p-14 rounded-[3rem] shadow-2xl">
      <div class="text-center mb-10">
        <h2 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-extrabold">Frequently Asked Questions (FAQ)</h2>
        <p class="text-mute mt-3 font-light text-lg">Resolving the most common doubts about the bulking phase and muscle gain.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="space-y-6 max-w-3xl mx-auto">
        
        <details class="group bg-surface-elevated/60 border border-hairline/50 p-6 md:p-8 rounded-3xl cursor-pointer hover:bg-surface-elevated transition-colors">
          <summary class="flex justify-between items-center font-bold text-xl outline-none">
            What exactly is a calorie surplus?
            <span class="transition-transform duration-300 group-open:rotate-180 flex-shrink-0 ml-4 text-blue-400">
              <svg fill="none" height="28" stroke="currentColor" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="28"><path d="M19 9l-7 7-7-7"></path></svg>
            </span>
          </summary>
          <p class="mt-6 text-mute font-light leading-relaxed text-lg border-t border-hairline pt-6">
            A calorie surplus means consuming more calories from macronutrients (proteins, fats, carbohydrates) than your body burns in total each day. When this constant excess energy is combined with a powerful strength training stimulus (weightlifting), the body uses that spare energy and amino acids to synthesize new muscle fibers.
          </p>
        </details>

        <details class="group bg-surface-elevated/60 border border-hairline/50 p-6 md:p-8 rounded-3xl cursor-pointer hover:bg-surface-elevated transition-colors">
          <summary class="flex justify-between items-center font-bold text-xl outline-none">
            How big should my calorie surplus be?
            <span class="transition-transform duration-300 group-open:rotate-180 flex-shrink-0 ml-4 text-blue-400">
              <svg fill="none" height="28" stroke="currentColor" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="28"><path d="M19 9l-7 7-7-7"></path></svg>
            </span>
          </summary>
          <p class="mt-6 text-mute font-light leading-relaxed text-lg border-t border-hairline pt-6">
            Sports science recommends a structured and moderate surplus of between 200 and 300 calories per day, depending on your experience. For beginners, a slightly larger surplus (+400 kcal) can be useful because of"Newbie Gains." Larger surpluses (+800 kcal) will add tons of body fat without building muscle any faster.
          </p>
        </details>

        <details class="group bg-surface-elevated/60 border border-hairline/50 p-6 md:p-8 rounded-3xl cursor-pointer hover:bg-surface-elevated transition-colors">
          <summary class="flex justify-between items-center font-bold text-xl outline-none">
            Can I gain muscle while in a calorie deficit?
            <span class="transition-transform duration-300 group-open:rotate-180 flex-shrink-0 ml-4 text-blue-400">
              <svg fill="none" height="28" stroke="currentColor" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="28"><path d="M19 9l-7 7-7-7"></path></svg>
            </span>
          </summary>
          <p class="mt-6 text-mute font-light leading-relaxed text-lg border-t border-hairline pt-6">
            Known as"Body Recomposition," gaining muscle and losing fat at the same time is only possible under very specific conditions: total beginners, people with severe obesity, or those returning to training after a break (muscle memory). For intermediate athletes, it's much better to alternate dedicated bulking and cutting phases.
          </p>
        </details>

      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Suggested Content Section & Ultimate CTA -->
    <section class="mt-32 pt-16 border-t-2 border-hairline">
      <div class="text-center mb-12">
        <h2 class="text-3xl md:text-5xl font-extrabold text-ink tracking-tight">Suggested Content</h2>
        <p class="text-body mt-4 text-xl font-light">Continue your learning by mastering these fundamental areas of fitness and sports nutrition.</p>
      </div>
      
      <div class="grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-8 mb-16">
         <!-- Related Guide 1 -->
         <a href="/guides/calorie-deficit-guide/" class="group block bg-surface rounded-[2.5rem] p-10 shadow-lg border border-hairline hover:shadow-2xl hover:border-hairline hover:-translate-y-2 transition-all duration-300">
           <div class="w-16 h-16 bg-brand-soft text-brand rounded-2xl flex items-center justify-center mb-8 group-hover:scale-110 transition-transform duration-300 shadow-sm">
             <svg class="w-8 h-8" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M13 17h8m0 0V9m0 8l-8-8-4 4-6-6"></path></svg>
           </div>
           <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-ink mb-4 group-hover:text-brand transition-colors">Calorie Deficit: The Complete Guide</h3>
           <p class="text-body leading-relaxed font-light text-lg">The other side of the coin. Learn the science of fat loss and discover how to calculate a precise calorie deficit to lean out your physique, shred abdominal fat, and keep all the muscle you gained.</p>
         </a>
         
